---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../../../styles/story.css";
import ReadingModes from "../../../components/ReadingModes.astro";
import Cite from "../../../components/Cite.astro";
import QuoteCard from "../../../components/story/QuoteCard.astro";
import DecisionTimeline from "../../../components/story/DecisionTimeline.astro";
import StorySources from "../../../components/StorySources.astro";
import { event, n, computed, maps, warnings } from "../../../data/iowa-army-detonation-2026-11-20-army-detonates-lead-azide-in-iowa.mjs";
const fmt = computed.fmt;
---
<BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/iowa-army-detonation/">Iowa Army detonation</a><span>November 20, 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="fact">Iowa Army detonation &middot; November 20, 2026 &middot; updated {event.updated}</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact" data-claims="army-nov24-time army-nov24-urge army-nov24-relocation lead-azide-what">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}<Cite c="army-nov24-relocation" /><Cite c="army-nov24-zone" /><Cite c="army-nov24-precise" /><Cite c="ca8-denies-stay" /><Cite c="telle-wed-morning" /></p>
      <ReadingModes />
      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-plume">Where the lead goes</a></li>
          <li><a href="#why-now">Why a blast, and why now</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-sides">The fight in court</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-catch">The Catch</a></li>
          <li><a href="#coverage">What the coverage got right and wrong</a></li>
          <li><a href="#next">What happens next</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
        </details>
      </nav>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">The Army plans to blow up {n.drums} drums of lead azide, an explosive used to set off ammunition, at the Iowa Army Ammunition Plant in Middletown, Iowa, "at approximately 9:00 a.m. CST" on Wednesday, Nov. 25, according to an advisory it published Nov. 24.<Cite c="army-nov24-time" /><Cite c="sixty-eight-drums" /><Cite c="lead-azide-what" /><Cite c="plant-location" /> It says a more precise time will be given after it weighs changing weather patterns.<Cite c="army-nov24-precise" /> The advisory says FEMA is supporting the recommended relocation of everyone in Middletown and the immediate surrounding areas, starting Nov. 24, and local and federal officials "strongly urge" people to leave.<Cite c="army-nov24-relocation" /><Cite c="army-nov24-urge" /> It defines the zone as a 5-mile cone extending southwest of the plant, based on modeling by the Defense Threat Reduction Agency, plus one mile beyond the modeled path of the plume.<Cite c="army-nov24-zone" /> It says the support includes rides for people who cannot travel on their own, shelter, food, and lodging through Friday, Nov. 27; people outside the zone are encouraged to shelter in place.<Cite c="army-nov24-support" /><Cite c="army-nov24-shelter" /> Anyone who cannot leave beforehand is told to "remain indoors during the potential exposure period" and keep windows and doors closed.<Cite c="army-nov24-cannot-leave" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">On Nov. 23, President Donald Trump said he had ordered the military not to carry out the blast until a federal appeals court agreed it could go forward.<Cite c="trump-ordered-hold" /> He said he was making the decision "fully subject to" Gov.-elect Rob Sand agreeing.<Cite c="trump-ordered-hold" /> The same day, the court lifted its pause on the Environmental Protection Agency's emergency permit for the blast.<Cite c="permit-authorizes-detonation" /> It denied two environmental groups, the Iowa Environmental Council and the Sierra Club, a stay of the permit while it hears their challenge, saying they had failed to show they are likely to prevail.<Cite c="ca8-denies-stay" /><Cite c="suit-stay-ask" /><Cite c="ca8-not-likely" /> That night, after the ruling, Acting Army Secretary Adam Telle said the detonation "will occur this Wednesday morning," Nov. 25, "as previously scheduled, based on forecast wind conditions."<Cite c="telle-wed-morning" /><Cite c="telle-post-time-nov23" /><Cite c="army-now-nov25" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Trump said he thinks the proper course is to get rid of the material, and that if Sand or the courts did not agree, the administration would not proceed and would hold the incoming governor responsible for any death or destruction.<Cite c="trump-proper-course" /><Cite c="trump-hold-sand-responsible" /> Sand said the administration "should make this decision based on the information they have from the people they brought in to do the job months ago and have been on the ground in Middletown for weeks."<Cite c="sand-decision-experts" /> He said he had asked the federal government to share with the public what he had been told in briefings.<Cite c="sand-asked-share" /> Telle's post points to the court's order and quotes Sand's statement, then says the Army is proceeding.<Cite c="telle-cites-sand" /> He wrote that it is the unanimous judgment of the Army, EPA, FBI, CDC and every other agency involved that the Army should proceed, that Gov. Kim Reynolds supports going forward, and that both Iowa's current and incoming governors "say the call belongs to the experts."<Cite c="telle-unanimous" /><Cite c="telle-governors" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The court said "the risk is between a controlled detonation and an unplanned explosion, not between the risk of a controlled detonation and no explosion at all."<Cite c="ca8-controlled-vs-unplanned" /> It said it acknowledges, and the parties agree, that the controlled detonation will result in harm to the public through environmental and health risks.<Cite c="ca8-harm-acknowledged" /> The court had paused the permit on Nov. 19.<Cite c="stay-granted" /> In a Nov. 21 notice, the government said the Army intends to act under an emergency exclusion in EPA's hazardous-waste rules for munitions and explosives, which it says lets the Army detonate without the permit.<Cite c="army-intends-by-nov24" /><Cite c="army-emergency-exclusion" /> The court said the Army is not a defendant in the case and that it does not opine on that plan.<Cite c="ca8-army-not-before-court" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The government's Nov. 21 notice says there is an immediate risk the lead azide will explode because temperatures are expected to drop below 30 degrees Fahrenheit in the next several days.<Cite c="army-cold-reason" /> The Army had moved the blast from Friday, Nov. 20, to Monday, Nov. 23.<Cite c="postponed-to-monday" /> EPA's opposition to the groups' motion, filed Nov. 22, says the Army then moved it to Nov. 24, and the government's notice says the Army intended to act "by November 24."<Cite c="epa-opposition-filed" /><Cite c="epa-postponed-to-24" /><Cite c="army-intends-by-nov24" /> The Army's notice published Nov. 22 moved it again, to Nov. 25, "to allow for more optimal weather conditions and all mitigation measures to be emplaced."<Cite c="army-now-nov25" /><Cite c="army-nov25-reason" /> EPA's opposition also asks the court to lift the pause.<Cite c="epa-asks-lift-stay" /> The same day, the groups asked the court to accept sworn statements, including from experts who question whether waiting for the court would make the material more dangerous.<Cite c="groups-supplement" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The drums hold about {fmt(n.pounds)} pounds of lead azide, by the count in a letter from Illinois and Iowa officials.<Cite c="weight-9700" /> In storage it crystallized and grew more sensitive to friction and shock, and the Army says moving it could set it off.<Cite c="crystallized-sensitive" /><Cite c="moving-risks-detonation" /> The drums sit in two earth-covered bunkers, and the Army plans to detonate both at once.<Cite c="one-per-bunker" /> The blasts had been set for about noon on Friday, Nov. 20, until Acting Army Secretary Adam Telle moved them on Thursday evening, saying Monday was the latest the Army could wait before a forecast drop in temperature that would risk making the material less stable.<Cite c="original-schedule" /><Cite c="postponed-to-monday" /><Cite c="telle-post-time" /><Cite c="telle-weather-reason" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">The Army says the blast will send up a plume with lead in it that disperses and settles on the ground.<Cite c="plume-contains-lead" /> It tells people in the affected area to stay inside.<Cite c="advisory-wet-clean" /> For Friday it had asked residents near two parks in Middletown to leave from 8 a.m. to 2 p.m., because the blast could break windows.<Cite c="city-park-vacate" /> Linn County's emergency office said the maps the Army drew for Friday's weather should not be used to judge the rescheduled blast.<Cite c="old-maps-not-monday" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">EPA's regional office issued the emergency permit that allows the detonation on Nov. 13.<Cite c="permit-dates" /><Cite c="permit-authorizes-detonation" /> The groups argue EPA approved the blast without fully considering less dangerous alternatives and took no public comment.<Cite c="suit-alternatives" /><Cite c="suit-no-comment" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-plume">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Where the lead goes</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">EPA's permit requires the Army's plume maps to assume that all of the lead is released, and EPA and the Army both say the maps are not necessarily what will happen; the Army expects less than all of it to get out because of the measures it will take to limit the blast's effects.<Cite c="maps-all-lead" /><Cite c="maps-not-representative" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">There are two maps: one of lead in the air, and one of lead dust settling on the ground, which draws two levels, a lighter and a heavier one.<Cite c="dust-two-levels" /> The air map's colored area ends where the modeled concentration falls to {n.edge} micrograms per cubic meter, which the map labels the smallest amount the Army's air samplers can detect.<Cite c="air-map-edge" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>How far the Army's maps drew the lead, for Friday's weather</strong></figcaption>
          <table class="map-table">
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Map</th><th scope="col">Lead in the air reached</th><th scope="col">Settling dust covered an area</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {maps.map((m) => <tr><th scope="row">{m.map}</th><td>{m.air}<Cite c="air-reach-100-to-60" /></td><td>{m.dust}<Cite c="deposition-25-40" /></td></tr>)}
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p class="chart-source">Air reach as The New York Times reported the Army's maps; dust areas by the environmental groups' reading of them.<Cite c="air-reach-100-to-60" /><Cite c="deposition-25-40" /></p>
        </figure>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Illinois EPA said on Nov. 18 that the Army's air modeling could reach Mercer County, Illinois, and that EPA's regional office for Iowa never told it about the blast.<Cite c="illinois-mercer" /><Cite c="illinois-not-notified" /> On Nov. 20, Illinois agencies said current weather information indicated the plume could reach western and central Illinois, and that Gov. JB Pritzker asked Telle for the plume modeling in a call that day.<Cite c="illinois-west-central" /><Cite c="pritzker-asked-modeling" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The Army says levels of lead in the air may be elevated but well below the level at which EPA would require protective action.<Cite c="well-below-claim" /> Its own environmental assessment says that in the worst case, airborne levels could pass the lowest of three tiers of protective-action criteria, the one at which people may feel temporary and reversible discomfort, but not the two higher tiers.<Cite c="ea-pac1" /> Asked by The Intercept what levels it expected in nearby communities, the Army gave no numbers.<Cite c="no-offsite-number" /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">What people are told to do</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The Army says the dust should linger no more than an hour, depending on the wind; Iowa's health department advises people in the affected areas to keep windows and doors shut for {n.windowsLowHours} to {n.windowsHighHours} hours after the blast, unless updated guidance says otherwise.<Cite c="linger-one-hour" /><Cite c="hhs-24-48" /> After that, the Army's written advice is to wash or wipe down with water what children touch and to consider taking the car through a car wash. It says the cleaning instructions should include specific procedures, but does not give them.<Cite c="advisory-wet-clean" /><Cite c="advisory-procedures-missing" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Justine Barati, a spokesperson for Army Sustainment Command, told Radio Iowa on Nov. 17:<Cite c="barati-hose" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="Justine Barati, Army Sustainment Command spokesperson" when="Nov. 17, 2026" where="Radio Iowa">
          <p>"The good thing is, if people have concerns about it being on their property, all they have to do is hose that off, and it’s gone."<Cite c="barati-hose" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Peter Thorne, a University of Iowa professor emeritus of environmental health, says hosing lead dust off cars and other surfaces moves it into the soil or into streams and ponds.<Cite c="thorne-hosing" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="why-now">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Why a blast, and why now</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The lead azide sat for about 20 years in the two bunkers, in a bath of ethanol and water meant to keep it stable, and an inspection in March found the bath had evaporated, Barati says.<Cite c="storage-20-years" /> The Army says it was military explosives-disposal staff who directed that the drums not be moved.<Cite c="do-not-move-directive" /> The Army's assessment says the crystals form most in cold months, below freezing.<Cite c="cold-worse" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The government's Nov. 21 notice says the crystals break more easily in freezing temperatures, raising the risk of an uncontrolled explosion, and that the bunkers are more exposed to temperature swings because their blast doors were removed to prepare for the detonation and cannot be replaced.<Cite c="army-freezing-crystals" /><Cite c="army-blast-doors" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The Army considered destroying the explosive with chemicals where it sits, but rejected that because it would mean {n.neutralizeLowDays} to {n.neutralizeHighDays} days of handling the drums, which it called an unacceptable risk to people.<Cite c="ea-neutralization-rejected" /> Its five criteria for choosing a method include how quickly the plant can get back to its work.<Cite c="ea-timeline-criterion" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Iowa Gov. Kim Reynolds says she spoke with Telle on Thursday, Nov. 19, and asked him for a delay, and eight Illinois and Iowa officials, among them Illinois Sens. Tammy Duckworth and Dick Durbin and Gov. JB Pritzker, had asked him in a letter to consider one so safer alternatives could be explored.<Cite c="telle-weather-reason" /><Cite c="reynolds-asked" /><Cite c="letter-delay" /><Cite c="letter-signers" /> Sen. Chuck Grassley said the Army was delaying "to further educate the public."<Cite c="grassley-educate" /> The environmental groups had filed suit hours before Telle's announcement.<Cite c="suit-hours-before" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-sides">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">The fight in court</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">EPA's rules treat an open blast as a way to get rid of explosive waste that cannot safely be disposed of another way, and say enclosed methods are preferred.<Cite c="epa-rule-cannot-safely" /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">The environmental groups</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The groups, represented by Earthjustice lawyers, argue the permit is unlawful.<Cite c="earthjustice-counsel" /> They say it leaves out the environmental performance standards the rules for open detonation require.<Cite c="suit-performance-standards" /> They say EPA accepted the Army's choice of an open blast without fully considering less dangerous alternatives, among them mobile treatment units and a chemical treatment called MuniRem, which they say EPA lists as having been used on lead azide.<Cite c="suit-alternatives" /> And they say EPA took no public comment.<Cite c="suit-no-comment" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">In an affidavit the groups filed Nov. 22, Mark J. Thomas, who worked at EPA from 1990 until he retired in 2019, says soil is an effective thermal insulator and that the forecast cold "is therefore comparable to conditions the stored material has already experienced this month without incident."<Cite c="thomas-who" /><Cite c="thomas-comparable" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The council's executive director, Sarah Green, said in the groups' announcement:<Cite c="groups-concede" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="Sarah Green, executive director, Iowa Environmental Council" when="Nov. 19, 2026" where="Earthjustice news release">
          <p>"It very well could be the case that the U.S. Army has no other safe alternative to disposing of the toxic lead azide."<Cite c="groups-concede" /></p>
          <p>"But our federal government needs to do better at communicating the potential health risks of lead contamination in our communities and providing more time to keep our communities and vulnerable groups safe, like children and pregnant women."<Cite c="groups-concede" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">The Army and EPA</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">EPA told The Intercept that the Army concluded the material cannot be safely handled or moved, and so cannot be treated any way but by blowing it up in place.<Cite c="epa-in-place-only" /> Rep. Mariannette Miller-Meeks of Iowa calls the controlled detonation the safest and best option.<Cite c="millermeeks-backs" /> The record that would show how the Army weighed the other methods is its analysis, sent to EPA in letters in August and September, which the groups say EPA has not made public.<Cite c="alternatives-analysis-not-public" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">In its Nov. 21 notice, the government argues that waiting is the greater danger:<Cite c="army-real-choice" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="U.S. Department of Justice, for EPA" when="Nov. 21, 2026" where="Notice to the Eighth Circuit">
          <p>"The real choice, in other words, is not between a detonation and no detonation. It is between a planned and mitigated detonation and an unplanned and unmitigated explosion."<Cite c="army-real-choice" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">The court</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Circuit Judges Shepherd, Grasz and Justin D. Smith issued the ruling as a published opinion.<Cite c="ca8-panel" /> It said EPA "adequately incorporated safeguards to minimize the dispersal of lead dust into the environment."<Cite c="ca8-safeguards" /> On alternatives, it said the record does not provide a sufficient basis to conclude that EPA failed to meaningfully consider safer ones.<Cite c="ca8-alternatives" /> On public comment, it said the 45-day comment period is not required before an emergency permit where it would be "inconsistent with the emergency situation."<Cite c="ca8-no-comment-ok" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The Catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>When did the Army publish its plume maps?<Cite c="first-maps-nov15" /></h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">Postponing the blast, Telle wrote:</p>
              <QuoteCard speaker="Adam Telle, acting secretary of the Army" when="Nov. 19, 2026" where="Post on X, republished by the Army">
                <p>"In March of this year, the Army uncovered degrading Lead Azide housed in bunkers at the installation and immediately began communicating with stakeholders and the public in Middletown and the modeled impact area about the danger it posed."<Cite c="telle-immediately" /></p>
              </QuoteCard>
              <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
                <DecisionTimeline entries={warnings} heading="What the Army said and did" />
              </figure>
              <p data-layer="narrative">EPA's permit required the first plume map at least five days before the blast, and Barati says the Army met that.<Cite c="permit-five-days" /><Cite c="barati-permit-five-days" /> On Nov. 17, she told Iowa Starting Line:<Cite c="barati-shocking" /></p>
              <QuoteCard speaker="Justine Barati, Army Sustainment Command spokesperson" when="Nov. 17, 2026" where="Interview with Iowa Starting Line">
                <p>"I understand that it’s shocking to the people that we haven’t told before."<Cite c="barati-shocking" /></p>
              </QuoteCard>
              <p data-layer="narrative">The Army published its first plume maps on Nov. 15, and the Iowa governor's office says it learned of the plan on Nov. 16.<Cite c="first-maps-nov15" /><Cite c="governor-learned-monday" /></p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="coverage">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What the coverage got right and wrong</h2>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">CBS News and KCRG: an indefinite pause, an injunction, or a stay until the court rules?</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">CBS News reported that a federal court order "paused the detonation plans indefinitely" and that the appeals court "granted an emergency motion to pause the demolition."<Cite c="cbs-indefinitely" /> KCRG reported that the appeals court "granted an emergency injunction to pause the lead azide detonation."<Cite c="kcrg-injunction" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The court's Nov. 19 order is an administrative stay that holds EPA's permit until the court rules on the groups' emergency motion.<Cite c="stay-granted" /> The court ruled on Nov. 23 and lifted it.<Cite c="ca8-denies-stay" /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">The Intercept, Nov. 19: overstated</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The Intercept wrote that the blast would end up "blanketing communities in lead-containing particles up to 100 miles away."<Cite c="intercept-blanketing" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The roughly 100 miles reported for the Army's first maps was lead in the air.<Cite c="air-reach-100-to-60" /> The air map's edge is the smallest amount the Army's samplers can detect, and the maps assume all the lead is released.<Cite c="air-map-edge" /><Cite c="maps-all-lead" /> By the environmental groups' reading, the settling dust covered a far smaller area, as the <a href="#the-plume">map table above</a> shows.<Cite c="deposition-25-40" /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">CBS News Chicago, Nov. 18: checks out, for the first maps</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">CBS News Chicago wrote that the plume was expected to spread across parts of eastern Iowa and northwest Illinois on Friday.<Cite c="cbs-nw-illinois" /> That matches what Illinois EPA said the same day of the Army's modeling, which it said could reach Mercer County.<Cite c="illinois-mercer" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="next">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happens next</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">EPA's permit, which the court says took effect Nov. 13, expires Feb. 11, 2027.<Cite c="ca8-permit-term" /> On the full challenge, the record of EPA's decision is due Dec. 29, and the groups' brief is due Jan. 12, 2027.<Cite c="briefing-schedule" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The permit requires the Army to publish an updated plume map {n.mapHoursBefore} hours ahead, then a new one every hour from {n.hourlyFromHours} hours ahead, and a final map within {n.finalMapHours} hours after.<Cite c="permit-map-schedule" /> It bars the blast in surface winds under {n.windLowMph} or over {n.windHighMph} miles per hour, in rain, or during a temperature inversion, when warmer air above traps air near the ground.<Cite c="permit-weather" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">On Nov. 22, Linn County's emergency office said Iowa's homeland security and health agencies still had no detonation date from the Army, and that the Army had not released updated plume modeling since an advisory dated Nov. 18.<Cite c="state-no-date" /><Cite c="linn-no-new-modeling" /> The Army's notice moving the blast to Nov. 25 says additional details will be forthcoming.<Cite c="army-now-nov25" /><Cite c="army-details-forthcoming" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">In a Nov. 23 notice to the court, the government said the Army chose Wednesday for its weather: the wind is "reasonably expected to be East to West/Southwest, and away from high population areas and larger water bodies," and the days after are predicted to be less favorable.<Cite c="notice-wind" /><Cite c="notice-later-worse" /> The notice says the Army is adding blast barriers around the bunkers, will apply thousands of gallons of water during the blast, and will first apply gels and foams from the Department of Energy, to limit the spread of lead dust.<Cite c="notice-mitigation" /> It cites a declaration by Maj. Gen. Eric P. Shirley for these points.<Cite c="notice-cites-shirley" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The Army's new maps, issued Nov. 24 for a 9 a.m. blast on Nov. 25, are labeled "mitigated," which the maps say reflects deliberate steps such as detonating in a bunker with added countermeasures.<Cite c="maps-nov24-mitigated" /> In his sworn declaration on Nov. 23, Shirley, the commander of Army Sustainment Command, said the added measures should cut the share of lead put into the air during the blast to 0.26 or lower; the new maps print 0.26 beside "Mitigated."<Cite c="shirley-emission-26" /><Cite c="maps-nov24-026" /> The dust map shows lead settling above EPA's maximum for windows, 100 micrograms per square foot, over 27 square miles, and above its maximum for floors, 5 micrograms, over 148 square miles.<Cite c="maps-nov24-areas" /> The air map shows the plume one, four and eight hours after the blast, out to the smallest amount the Army's samplers can detect.<Cite c="maps-nov24-hours" /> On Nov. 24, Linn County's emergency office said the new maps put the projected area of impact southwest of the plant and Linn County outside it, and that, under current forecasts and state health advice, people outside that area do not need to take protective steps.<Cite c="linn-nov24-southwest" /><Cite c="linn-nov24-no-action" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Within {n.samplingHours} hours after the blast, the Army must start testing soil and wiping surfaces for lead at parks, public places and private properties whose owners agreed, but only inside the heavier of the final dust map's two levels.<Cite c="permit-sampling" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="records">
      <StorySources event={event.slug} />
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .map-table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .map-table th, .map-table td { text-align: left; padding: 0.5rem 0.6rem 0.5rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); vertical-align: top; color: var(--ink); }
  .map-table thead th { font-size: 0.78rem; color: var(--slate); font-weight: 600; }
</style>
